Source: mptcp-bpf-weight-rr
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), clang, llvm, libbpf-dev, linux-libc-dev
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://www.openmptcprouter.com
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: mptcp-bpf-weight-rr
Architecture: any
# No ${shlibs:Depends}: the installed artifact is a BPF-target ELF object
# loaded by bpftool/the kernel, not a dynamically-linked host executable -
# see mptcp-bpf-dscp's identical note.
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, bpftool, procps
Description: MPTCP BPF weighted round-robin scheduler (server/VPS side)
 BPF struct_ops MPTCP scheduler that distributes send bursts across ALL
 active subflows proportionally to weight (smooth weighted round-robin),
 instead of always picking a single winner like mptcp-bpf-weight -
 higher weight = more traffic, but every active WAN still carries some.
 Weighted either by local endpoint IP (router-side, see the OpenWrt
 mptcp-bpf-weight-rr package in openmptcprouter-feeds) or by MPTCP
 remote endpoint id (server/VPS-side: every subflow here shares the
 same local IP, so weights key on the address id the router assigned
 to the originating WAN instead).
 .
 This is the Debian/server counterpart of that OpenWrt package: same
 BPF program, packaged to run on the VPS half of an OpenMPTCProuter
 multipath link. Ships a systemd unit that loads and pins the
 scheduler at boot and sets it as net.mptcp.scheduler. Install
 mptcp-weight-manager (from the mptcp-bpf-weight source package) to
 actually configure weights.
